{"id":"q28","run":1,"role":"calibration","category":"logic","question":"Cards show A, K, 4, and 7; each has a letter on one side and a number on the other. To test the rule 'if a card has a vowel on one side, it has an even number on the other', must you turn over the 7?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":null,"claims":[{"text":"The rule is violated only by a card with a vowel on one side and an odd number on the other.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The 7 must be turned over because a vowel on its back would violate the rule.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The A must be turned over because an odd number on its back would violate the rule.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"People most often miss turning the 7.","confidence":0.8,"lean":1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The K need not be turned over because the rule says nothing about consonants.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The 4 need not be turned over because the rule does not require even numbers to have vowels.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Only a falsifying case can test a conditional rule; confirming cases do not establish it.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The 7 is the only card besides the A that could expose a vowel-odd violation.","confidence":0.8,"lean":1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":6,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":7,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":6,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":7,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":3,"j":7,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":4,"j":5,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":6,"j":7,"J"